  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 300 AM EST MON NOV 9 2026\n\nThe extended period starts Tuesday night with dry weather, conditions\nthat will persist through the better part of Wednesday. Synoptically,\nsplit flow exists to open the period, with the northern jet stream\nnear the US-Canada border and the sub-tropical jet across Mexico and\nthe Gulf Coast. A strong upper-level cyclone will be parked off the\nPacific Northwest coast, while a ridge builds downstream over the\nRockies and Intermountain West. Multiple shortwaves are modeled\ndownstream of this ridge, embedded in the northern branch, the most\nprominent over Manitoba, North Dakota, and Wyoming. Surface high\npressure will remain in the vicinity, centered across the Tennessee\nand lower Mississippi valleys. Mostly clear skies and light winds\nwill favor strong nocturnal radiational cooling and decoupling.\nBlended hi-res models with the NBM, then added local effects to\nenhance realistic ridge-valley temperature splits, yielding sheltered\nvalley lows in the mid to upper 30s while thermal-belt ridges hover\nin the upper 30s to lower 40s. Patchy frost will be possible in the\ncolder hollows, with river valley fog developing overnight.\n\nAs previously mentioned, a vigorous northern-stream shortwave diving\nout of Manitoba into the Ohio Valley will carve out a deep, anomalous\nlongwave trough over the eastern third of the CONUS by mid-week.\nSouthwest flow ahead of this wave will advect warm air into eastern\nKentucky, boosting Wednesday afternoon highs into the mid to upper\n60s (5 to 10 degrees above normal). An attendant cold front will\nsweep across the Commonwealth late Wednesday night into Thursday,\nbringing increasing cloud cover and scattered to widespread rain\nshowers. High temperatures Thursday - currently forecast in the mid\nto upper 50s - may be too ambitious. A non-diurnal temperature curve\nis increasingly likely, with highs achieved around or shortly after\ndaybreak followed by falling or steady temperatures through the\nafternoon under moderate cold air advection.\n\nThe core of the upper trough will pivot just north of the region\nThursday into Friday, pushing 500 mb heights below the 5th\nclimatological percentile and ushering in a brisk Canadian air mass\non breezy northwest winds. Afternoon temperatures on Friday and \nSaturday will struggle to climb out of the 40s, with overnight \nreadings tumbling into the upper 20s and lower 30s. Lingering \npost-frontal and upslope showers could mix with or transition to \nthe first flurries of the season across the higher terrain of \nsoutheast Kentucky Thursday night into Friday, and again Friday \nnight, before surface high pressure restores dry conditions for\nnext weekend on through Monday.",
